UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 15: Personal finance and budgeting', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"When money is limited, needs spending (fares, lunch, required materials) is planned before wants spending (games, extra merch). Priority order does not mean wants are bad; it means needs are secured first so you can still get to school and eat.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same HSIE idea to a new place or source, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nPriority order for $30: $12 required workbook, $9 lunch, $15 concert pin. What is bought first and what waits?\n\nFEEDBACK\nWorkbook and lunch are needs ($21). Pin is a want. After needs, $9 remains, so the pin waits unless the budget increases.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBuying the want first “to feel better” and hoping needs somehow shrink. Needs do not shrink because a want felt urgent.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a $40 plan with two needs and one want. Show the needs total, the remainder, and whether the want fits. Use new amounts.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one source or idea you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Commerce","learning_intention":"When money is limited, needs spending (fares, lunch, required materials) is planned before wants spending (games, extra merch). Priority order does not mean wants are bad; it means needs are secured first so you can still get to school and eat.","lesson_number":15,"lesson_role":"Learn","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: local map, photo or interview notes"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or 