planation":"When money is limited, needs spending (fares, lunch, required materials) is planned before wants spending (games, extra merch).","options":["When money is limited, needs spending (fares, lunch, required materials) is planned before wants spending (games, extra","A claim unrelated to the source or place","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about needs spending?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nJordan has $25. Bus fare $10 and lunch $8 are needs ($18). A $12 poster is a want. After needs, $25 − $18 = $7 left, which is not enough for the poster. The want waits.\n\nTRY FIRST\nPriority order for $30: $12 required workbook, $9 lunch, $15 concert pin. What is bought first and what waits?\n\nFEEDBACK\nWorkbook and lunch are needs ($21). Pin is a want. After needs, $9 remains, so the pin waits unless the budget increases.","explain":"When money is limited, needs spending (fares, lunch, required materials) is planned before wants spending (games, extra merch). Priority order does not mean wants are bad; it means needs are secured first so you can still get to school and eat.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on sources, places and respectful community knowledge, rather than guessing from a single picture. Keep every claim linked to evidence.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the sources, map features or community examples that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a detail is uncertain, identify what source you would need next. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Buying the want first “to feel better” and hoping needs somehow shrink. Needs do not shrink because a want felt urgent. Correct 